## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical Android data layer skills: Room, Retrofit, coroutines, Flow, and offline-first UX. Focus is on implementing a simple, robust data path with graceful fallback and deterministic refresh behavior.

## Key Concepts
- Room data access objects and migrations
- Retrofit API interfaces and JSON mapping
- Repository pattern with Flow/StateFlow
- Offline-first data synchronization and error handling
- ViewModel integration and simple UI trigger

## Code Example
```kotlin
// Data model
@Entity(tableName = "notes")
data class Note(
  @PrimaryKey val id: String,
  val title: String,
  val content: String,
  val updatedAt: Long
)

// DAO sketch
@Dao
interface NoteDao {
  @Query("SELECT * FROM notes ORDER BY updatedAt DESC")
  fun observeNotes(): Flow<List<Note>>

  @Insert(onConflict = OnConflictStrategy.REPLACE)
  suspend fun upsert(notes: List<Note>)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions between local and remote updates?
- What backoff strategy would you use for network retries and why?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model data, persist with Room, export data offline, and share via intents. Emphasizes file access patterns and compatibility.

## Key Concepts
- Room: Entity, DAO, and a simple Repository
- JSON serialization of data
- FileProvider and content URIs
- Sharing via ACTION_SEND
- Runtime permissions considerations on older Android versions

## Code Example
```kotlin
@Entity(tableName = "notes")
data class Note(
  @PrimaryKey val id: Int,
  val title: String,
  val content: String,
  val createdAt: Long
)

@Dao interface NoteDao { @Query("SELECT * FROM notes ORDER BY createdAt DESC") fun getAllNotes(): List<Note> }

class NoteRepository(private val context: Context, private val dao: NoteDao) {
  fun exportNotes(): Uri {
    val notes = dao.getAllNotes()
    val json = Gson().toJson(notes)
    val dir = context.getExternalFilesDir(null)!!
    val file = File(dir, "notes.json")
    file.writeText(json)
    return FileProvider.getUriForFile(context, "com.example.fileprovider", file)
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle large note datasets to avoid O(n) memory usage during export?
- How would you test FileProvider permissions and share intent across Android versions?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes real-world offline-first sync patterns, deterministic conflict resolution, and Android tooling (Room, WorkManager). It tests handling intermittent connectivity, battery constraints, and data consistency.

## Key Concepts
- Room model with lastModified, serverVersion, and dirty flags
- WorkManager with constraints and exponential backoff
- Conflict resolution: last-writer-wins with a deterministic tie-breaker
- End-to-end testing: MockWebServer, offline simulation, and backoff verification

## Code Example
```javascript
function resolveNoteConflict(local, remote) {
  if (local.lastModified > remote.lastModified) return local;
  if (remote.lastModified > local.lastModified) return remote;
  // equal timestamps -> use serverVersion as tie-breaker
  return remote.serverVersion >= local.serverVersion ? remote : local;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle partial failures of pushes or streaming updates?
- How would you adapt the model for collaborative edits from multiple devices?

